      "retrieved_chunk_count": 10,
      "answer": "Based on the provided excerpts, the main motivation for studying containerised Linux edge nodes is that **edge deployments frequently place latency-sensitive inference services on the same commodity Linux nodes as housekeeping tasks** (sensor acquisition, logging, telemetry, connectivity management), and when these tasks together demand more processor time than available, **the achievable response time of the model is determined by the kernel scheduler (CFS) rather than by the model itself** [2]. \n\nExcerpt [1] further explains that these nodes are shared — \"alongside the model, the same processor drives acquisition loops, a message broker, log rotation, connectivity management and periodic maintenance\" — yet much of the edge AI literature evaluates models as though they had the device to themselves [1]. The study focuses on stock Linux kernels with CFS and cgroups v2 resource control, with inference co-resident with non-AI background work [4].",
      "has_sufficient_context": true,
      "answer_metrics": {
